## Product fixes made during e2e (real bugs, keep!)
- `src/tui/app.tsx`: `/quit` (and `/exit`) at an APPROVAL prompt now denies
  the pending tool call and exits — previously the user was stuck (input
  locked while approving, /quit unreachable). Regression test in
  `test/tui-app.test.tsx`.
- `src/tui/run.tsx`: `/quit` while BUSY now aborts the running turn and
  exits 0 (previously the busy guard swallowed it and its characters sat in
  the input line, looking dead). `mounted` guard prevents rerender-after-
  unmount (late agent_end after a /quit-abort). `TUI_DEBUG=1` logs every
  key event (pty → node stderr → capture file).
- `test/eval.ts`: scoring tolerates benign leading exploration calls — the
  27B legitimately does read→edit for fix-typo; the first call matching the
  expected tool is scored (previously calls[0] only → false failures).

## Harness fixes (test/e2e.sh + test/e2e-feederlib.sh)
- **CRITICAL — eval quoting bug**: the feeder functions were embedded in a
  single-quoted FEEDER_LIB string then `eval`'d inside a DOUBLE-QUOTED
  `bash -c` argument. Quote-state desync silently mangled the code
  (`printf "/quit\r"` became `printf /quitr` → every /quit lost its Enter;
  `case ... | ...` patterns became syntax errors). Fixed by moving the
  functions to `test/e2e-feederlib.sh`, SOURCED by the feeder bash (a real
  file parse). Verified with a functional test (printf preserves \r, all
  functions work).
- **Feeder blindness**: plain `new_since "y approve"` loops go blind when the
  run finishes first (the next wait blocks the whole budget). Added
  `approval_or_done` — polls the approval prompt AND the session for turn
  completion in one loop (rc 0=approval, 1=turn done, 2=timeout).
- **Session-based turn detection** (`turn_done`/`wait_turn_done`): a turn is
  done when the last session message is a final assistant message (no
  toolCall content blocks). Disc-based, immune to render-timing quirks.
  Resume runs need `wait_new_user_msg` first (a resumed session already ends
  in the previous turn's final assistant message → turn_done is instantly
  true; the feeder used to quit before the new turn started).
- **Stall detector** (the "clearly dead vs just slow" distinction the user
  asked for): `stall_check` fails fast (rc 99 + kills the app) after
  STALL_LIMIT (default 180s) of TOTAL silence — capture static, session
  static, AND the LLM server logging nothing. Any growth or provable server
  activity resets the clock, so slow model calls are tolerated. `server_busy`
  is kubectl-throttled (15s) and timeout-guarded (8s) so a hung cluster call
  never hangs the feeder.
- **Reliable exit codes**: macOS `script` returns 0 even when its child is
  SIGKILLed, so pty_feed now reads a feeder-written `<out>.status` file
  (clean-exit→0, stall→99, gave-up→1; missing→pipeline rc, 137=watchdog).
  quit_retry uses a subshell printf so a SIGPIPE kills the subshell, not the
  feeder (previously the feeder died mid-diagnostic).
- **`guarded_run` reconstructed** (it was lost in the FEEDER_LIB block
  removal — not in git, not in snapshots): watchdog + capture to out.log /
  out2.log (when out.log exists) + return the command rc.
- All TUI scenario watchdogs = 480s; internal budgets rebalanced to fit.
- `FEED_DEBUG=1` gives timestamped feeder traces (sent /quit, MATCH, turn
  done, STALL).

## Known environmental gotcha — SERVER CONTENTION
The e2e suite and the pi agent share the SAME single-model llama.cpp server.
A long pi session re-evals its full context (~38k tokens ≈ 68s prompt eval
per turn) and can queue/STARVE test requests (this is what killed the
repl-piped scenario in run 4: 480s with the REPL's first turn stuck behind
agent turns). Implications:
- Run the suite when the agent is idle (or accept long runtimes).
- The stall detector's server_busy check correctly tolerates this (server
  busy = someone is computing = keep waiting, don't declare a stall).
- Short agent turns while the suite runs help; long ones hurt.

